{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async throttling keeps the timing behavior described in the "},{"type":"link","href":"./throttling.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Throttling Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":", while adding Promise results, retries, error callbacks, and control over in-flight work."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use it when a throttled operation returns a value you need, can reject, or needs retry and abort support. A synchronous "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Throttler"},{"type":"text","value":" can invoke an async function as a side effect, but it does not manage the resulting Promise."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"quick-start","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quick start"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"asyncThrottle"},{"type":"text","value":" when you only need a callable function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { asyncThrottle } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst savePosition = asyncThrottle(\n  async (position: number) => {\n    const response = await fetch('/api/position', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      body: JSON.stringify({ position }),\n    })\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Save failed')\n    return response.json()\n  },\n  { wait: 1000 },\n)\n\nconst result = await savePosition(42)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncThrottler"},{"type":"text","value":" when you need methods, state, or callbacks:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { AsyncThrottler } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst saver = new AsyncThrottler(savePositionToServer, {\n  wait: 1000,\n  onSuccess: (result, args) => {\n    console.log('Saved position:', args[0], result)\n  },\n  onError: (error, args) => {\n    console.error('Save failed:', args[0], error)\n  },\n})\n\nconst result = await saver.maybeExecute(42)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default edge behavior is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leading: true"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"trailing: true"},{"type":"text","value":". The first call executes immediately. Calls during the interval update the arguments retained for one trailing execution."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"promise-results","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Promise results"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maybeExecute()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a Promise. An immediate or trailing execution resolves with its result. When another call replaces pending trailing work, the older pending Promise resolves with the throttler's current "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastResult"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"call A ─── execute A ─── result A\n                  call B ───┐\n                            ├─ call C replaces B ─── execute C\nPromise B ──────────────────┘ resolves with result A\nPromise C ────────────────────────────────────────── resolves with result C","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The replaced call does not wait for the newer trailing execution. If every call needs its own execution and result, use an "},{"type":"link","href":"./async-queuing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async Queue"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An async throttler also avoids starting its next scheduled execution while the current execution is still active. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" interval still controls throttle timing, while the Promise lifecycle can delay when later work is scheduled."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"leading-and-trailing-execution","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leading and trailing execution"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The edge combinations match synchronous throttling:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leading"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"trailing"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behavior"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Execute immediately and retain the latest call for one trailing execution. This is the default."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Execute immediately and discard calls made during the interval."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wait one interval before the first execution, then retain the latest call in each interval."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Record calls without executing the function."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unlike debouncing, calls during the interval do not restart the interval. They only replace the pending trailing arguments."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"errors-and-callbacks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Errors and callbacks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async throttlers provide callbacks around each actual execution:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSuccess(result, args, throttler)"},{"type":"text","value":" runs after success."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError(error, args, throttler)"},{"type":"text","value":" runs after the retries for an execution fail."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSettled(args, throttler)"},{"type":"text","value":" runs after either outcome."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", so a failure rejects the Promise that owns the execution. Providing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" changes that default to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":"; the Promise then resolves with the current "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastResult"},{"type":"text","value":". Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" explicitly to override the default."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Callbacks describe executions, not every call to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maybeExecute()"},{"type":"text","value":". Replaced or discarded calls do not produce execution callbacks."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"retrying-failed-executions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retrying failed executions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure the retryer used for each execution with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"asyncRetryerOptions"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const saver = new AsyncThrottler(savePositionToServer, {\n  wait: 1000,\n  asyncRetryerOptions: {\n    maxAttempts: 3,\n    backoff: 'exponential',\n    baseWait: 500,\n    jitter: 0.2,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxAttempts"},{"type":"text","value":" includes the first attempt. Throttling controls logical executions; retrying controls the attempts within each execution. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./async-retrying.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async Retrying Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" before enabling retries for operations with side effects."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"canceling-pending-work-and-aborting-active-work","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Canceling pending work and aborting active work"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel()"},{"type":"text","value":" clears a pending trailing execution. It does not stop active work or reset the current throttle interval."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"abort()"},{"type":"text","value":" aborts active executions. It does not clear pending trailing work."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" runs pending trailing work immediately and returns its result."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass the throttler's signal to the underlying API when it supports cancellation:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const saver = new AsyncThrottler(\n  async (position: number) => {\n    return fetch('/api/position', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      body: JSON.stringify({ position }),\n      signal: saver.getAbortSignal() ?? undefined,\n    })\n  },\n  { wait: 1000 },\n)\n\nsaver.abort()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"abort()"},{"type":"text","value":" without using the signal stops retry management but cannot force an arbitrary Promise to stop."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"resetting-safely","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resetting safely"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" restores default state, but it does not clear a scheduled timeout or guarantee that active work stops. Clean up the lifecycle first when necessary:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"saver.cancel()\nsaver.abort()\nsaver.reset()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"},{"type":"text","value":" may be values or functions that receive the throttler instance. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" merges new options into the existing configuration."}]},{"type":"code","value":"saver.setOptions({\n  enabled: (throttler) => throttler.store.state.errorCount < 3,\n  wait: (throttler) =>\n    throttler.store.state.successCount < 10 ? 500 : 1000,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A changed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" value does not reschedule existing trailing work. It applies to later scheduling and executions. Disabling the throttler through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" cancels pending trailing work."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"asyncThrottlerOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" to define reusable, type-checked option objects."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The class stores state at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throttler.store"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To restore selected state that your app has persisted, pass a partial snapshot through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":". It is merged with the defaults. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers and active executions are not restored."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Commonly useful properties include:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPending"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether a trailing execution is scheduled."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isExecuting"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether the wrapped function is active."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastArgs"},{"type":"text","value":": The latest arguments retained for trailing work."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastResult"},{"type":"text","value":": The most recent successful result."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastExecutionTime"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nextExecutionTime"},{"type":"text","value":": Current timing boundaries."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"successCount"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errorCount"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"settleCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Execution outcome counts."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../../../reference/classes/AsyncThrottler.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncThrottler"},{"type":"text","value":" API reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the complete state and option types."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"quick-start","text":"Quick start","level":2},{"id":"promise-results","text":"Promise results","level":2},{"id":"leading-and-trailing-execution","text":"Leading and trailing execution","level":2},{"id":"errors-and-callbacks","text":"Errors and callbacks","level":2},{"id":"retrying-failed-executions","text":"Retrying failed executions","level":2},{"id":"canceling-pending-work-and-aborting-active-work","text":"Canceling pending work and aborting active work","level":2},{"id":"resetting-safely","text":"Resetting safely","level":3},{"id":"configuration","text":"Configuration","level":2},{"id":"state","text":"State","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nAsync throttling keeps the timing behavior described in the [Throttling Guide](./throttling.md), while adding Promise results, retries, error callbacks, and control over in-flight work.\n\nUse it when a throttled operation returns a value you need, can reject, or needs retry and abort support. A synchronous `Throttler` can invoke an async function as a side effect, but it does not manage the resulting Promise.\n\n## Quick start\n\nUse `asyncThrottle` when you only need a callable function:\n\n```ts\nimport { asyncThrottle } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst savePosition = asyncThrottle(\n  async (position: number) => {\n    const response = await fetch('/api/position', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      body: JSON.stringify({ position }),\n    })\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Save failed')\n    return response.json()\n  },\n  { wait: 1000 },\n)\n\nconst result = await savePosition(42)\n```\n\nUse `AsyncThrottler` when you need methods, state, or callbacks:\n\n```ts\nimport { AsyncThrottler } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst saver = new AsyncThrottler(savePositionToServer, {\n  wait: 1000,\n  onSuccess: (result, args) => {\n    console.log('Saved position:', args[0], result)\n  },\n  onError: (error, args) => {\n    console.error('Save failed:', args[0], error)\n  },\n})\n\nconst result = await saver.maybeExecute(42)\n```\n\nThe default edge behavior is `leading: true` and `trailing: true`. The first call executes immediately. Calls during the interval update the arguments retained for one trailing execution.\n\n## Promise results\n\n`maybeExecute()` returns a Promise. An immediate or trailing execution resolves with its result. When another call replaces pending trailing work, the older pending Promise resolves with the throttler's current `lastResult`:\n\n```text\ncall A ─── execute A ─── result A\n                  call B ───┐\n                            ├─ call C replaces B ─── execute C\nPromise B ──────────────────┘ resolves with result A\nPromise C ────────────────────────────────────────── resolves with result C\n```\n\nThe replaced call does not wait for the newer trailing execution. If every call needs its own execution and result, use an [Async Queue](./async-queuing.md).\n\nAn async throttler also avoids starting its next scheduled execution while the current execution is still active. The `wait` interval still controls throttle timing, while the Promise lifecycle can delay when later work is scheduled.\n\n## Leading and trailing execution\n\nThe edge combinations match synchronous throttling:\n\n| `leading` | `trailing` | Behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `true` | `true` | Execute immediately and retain the latest call for one trailing execution. This is the default. |\n| `true` | `false` | Execute immediately and discard calls made during the interval. |\n| `false` | `true` | Wait one interval before the first execution, then retain the latest call in each interval. |\n| `false` | `false` | Record calls without executing the function. |\n\nUnlike debouncing, calls during the interval do not restart the interval. They only replace the pending trailing arguments.\n\n## Errors and callbacks\n\nAsync throttlers provide callbacks around each actual execution:\n\n- `onSuccess(result, args, throttler)` runs after success.\n- `onError(error, args, throttler)` runs after the retries for an execution fail.\n- `onSettled(args, throttler)` runs after either outcome.\n\nWithout `onError`, `throwOnError` defaults to `true`, so a failure rejects the Promise that owns the execution. Providing `onError` changes that default to `false`; the Promise then resolves with the current `lastResult`. Set `throwOnError` explicitly to override the default.\n\nCallbacks describe executions, not every call to `maybeExecute()`. Replaced or discarded calls do not produce execution callbacks.\n\n## Retrying failed executions\n\nConfigure the retryer used for each execution with `asyncRetryerOptions`:\n\n```ts\nconst saver = new AsyncThrottler(savePositionToServer, {\n  wait: 1000,\n  asyncRetryerOptions: {\n    maxAttempts: 3,\n    backoff: 'exponential',\n    baseWait: 500,\n    jitter: 0.2,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`maxAttempts` includes the first attempt. Throttling controls logical executions; retrying controls the attempts within each execution. See the [Async Retrying Guide](./async-retrying.md) before enabling retries for operations with side effects.\n\n## Canceling pending work and aborting active work\n\n- `cancel()` clears a pending trailing execution. It does not stop active work or reset the current throttle interval.\n- `abort()` aborts active executions. It does not clear pending trailing work.\n- `flush()` runs pending trailing work immediately and returns its result.\n\nPass the throttler's signal to the underlying API when it supports cancellation:\n\n```ts\nconst saver = new AsyncThrottler(\n  async (position: number) => {\n    return fetch('/api/position', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      body: JSON.stringify({ position }),\n      signal: saver.getAbortSignal() ?? undefined,\n    })\n  },\n  { wait: 1000 },\n)\n\nsaver.abort()\n```\n\nCalling `abort()` without using the signal stops retry management but cannot force an arbitrary Promise to stop.\n\n### Resetting safely\n\n`reset()` restores default state, but it does not clear a scheduled timeout or guarantee that active work stops. Clean up the lifecycle first when necessary:\n\n```ts\nsaver.cancel()\nsaver.abort()\nsaver.reset()\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\n`wait` and `enabled` may be values or functions that receive the throttler instance. `setOptions()` merges new options into the existing configuration.\n\n```ts\nsaver.setOptions({\n  enabled: (throttler) => throttler.store.state.errorCount < 3,\n  wait: (throttler) =>\n    throttler.store.state.successCount < 10 ? 500 : 1000,\n})\n```\n\nA changed `wait` value does not reschedule existing trailing work. It applies to later scheduling and executions. Disabling the throttler through `setOptions()` cancels pending trailing work.\n\nUse `asyncThrottlerOptions()` to define reusable, type-checked option objects.\n\n## State\n\nThe class stores state at `throttler.store`.\n\nTo restore selected state that your app has persisted, pass a partial snapshot through `initialState`. It is merged with the defaults. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers and active executions are not restored.\n\nCommonly useful properties include:\n\n- `isPending`: Whether a trailing execution is scheduled.\n- `isExecuting`: Whether the wrapped function is active.\n- `lastArgs`: The latest arguments retained for trailing work.\n- `lastResult`: The most recent successful result.\n- `lastExecutionTime` and `nextExecutionTime`: Current timing boundaries.\n- `successCount`, `errorCount`, and `settleCount`: Execution outcome counts.\n\nSee the [`AsyncThrottler` API reference](../../../reference/classes/AsyncThrottler.md) for the complete state and option types.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Vanilla Async Throttling Guide\nid: async-throttling\n---\n\nAsync throttling keeps the timing behavior described in the [Throttling Guide](./throttling.md), while adding Promise results, retries, error callbacks, and control over in-flight work.\n\nUse it when a throttled operation returns a value you need, can reject, or needs retry and abort support. A synchronous `Throttler` can invoke an async function as a side effect, but it does not manage the resulting Promise.\n\n## Quick start\n\nUse `asyncThrottle` when you only need a callable function:\n\n```ts\nimport { asyncThrottle } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst savePosition = asyncThrottle(\n  async (position: number) => {\n    const response = await fetch('/api/position', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      body: JSON.stringify({ position }),\n    })\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Save failed')\n    return response.json()\n  },\n  { wait: 1000 },\n)\n\nconst result = await savePosition(42)\n```\n\nUse `AsyncThrottler` when you need methods, state, or callbacks:\n\n```ts\nimport { AsyncThrottler } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst saver = new AsyncThrottler(savePositionToServer, {\n  wait: 1000,\n  onSuccess: (result, args) => {\n    console.log('Saved position:', args[0], result)\n  },\n  onError: (error, args) => {\n    console.error('Save failed:', args[0], error)\n  },\n})\n\nconst result = await saver.maybeExecute(42)\n```\n\nThe default edge behavior is `leading: true` and `trailing: true`. The first call executes immediately. Calls during the interval update the arguments retained for one trailing execution.\n\n## Promise results\n\n`maybeExecute()` returns a Promise. An immediate or trailing execution resolves with its result. When another call replaces pending trailing work, the older pending Promise resolves with the throttler's current `lastResult`:\n\n```text\ncall A ─── execute A ─── result A\n                  call B ───┐\n                            ├─ call C replaces B ─── execute C\nPromise B ──────────────────┘ resolves with result A\nPromise C ────────────────────────────────────────── resolves with result C\n```\n\nThe replaced call does not wait for the newer trailing execution. If every call needs its own execution and result, use an [Async Queue](./async-queuing.md).\n\nAn async throttler also avoids starting its next scheduled execution while the current execution is still active. The `wait` interval still controls throttle timing, while the Promise lifecycle can delay when later work is scheduled.\n\n## Leading and trailing execution\n\nThe edge combinations match synchronous throttling:\n\n| `leading` | `trailing` | Behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `true` | `true` | Execute immediately and retain the latest call for one trailing execution. This is the default. |\n| `true` | `false` | Execute immediately and discard calls made during the interval. |\n| `false` | `true` | Wait one interval before the first execution, then retain the latest call in each interval. |\n| `false` | `false` | Record calls without executing the function. |\n\nUnlike debouncing, calls during the interval do not restart the interval. They only replace the pending trailing arguments.\n\n## Errors and callbacks\n\nAsync throttlers provide callbacks around each actual execution:\n\n- `onSuccess(result, args, throttler)` runs after success.\n- `onError(error, args, throttler)` runs after the retries for an execution fail.\n- `onSettled(args, throttler)` runs after either outcome.\n\nWithout `onError`, `throwOnError` defaults to `true`, so a failure rejects the Promise that owns the execution. Providing `onError` changes that default to `false`; the Promise then resolves with the current `lastResult`. Set `throwOnError` explicitly to override the default.\n\nCallbacks describe executions, not every call to `maybeExecute()`. Replaced or discarded calls do not produce execution callbacks.\n\n## Retrying failed executions\n\nConfigure the retryer used for each execution with `asyncRetryerOptions`:\n\n```ts\nconst saver = new AsyncThrottler(savePositionToServer, {\n  wait: 1000,\n  asyncRetryerOptions: {\n    maxAttempts: 3,\n    backoff: 'exponential',\n    baseWait: 500,\n    jitter: 0.2,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`maxAttempts` includes the first attempt. Throttling controls logical executions; retrying controls the attempts within each execution. See the [Async Retrying Guide](./async-retrying.md) before enabling retries for operations with side effects.\n\n## Canceling pending work and aborting active work\n\n- `cancel()` clears a pending trailing execution. It does not stop active work or reset the current throttle interval.\n- `abort()` aborts active executions. It does not clear pending trailing work.\n- `flush()` runs pending trailing work immediately and returns its result.\n\nPass the throttler's signal to the underlying API when it supports cancellation:\n\n```ts\nconst saver = new AsyncThrottler(\n  async (position: number) => {\n    return fetch('/api/position', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      body: JSON.stringify({ position }),\n      signal: saver.getAbortSignal() ?? undefined,\n    })\n  },\n  { wait: 1000 },\n)\n\nsaver.abort()\n```\n\nCalling `abort()` without using the signal stops retry management but cannot force an arbitrary Promise to stop.\n\n### Resetting safely\n\n`reset()` restores default state, but it does not clear a scheduled timeout or guarantee that active work stops. Clean up the lifecycle first when necessary:\n\n```ts\nsaver.cancel()\nsaver.abort()\nsaver.reset()\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\n`wait` and `enabled` may be values or functions that receive the throttler instance. `setOptions()` merges new options into the existing configuration.\n\n```ts\nsaver.setOptions({\n  enabled: (throttler) => throttler.store.state.errorCount < 3,\n  wait: (throttler) =>\n    throttler.store.state.successCount < 10 ? 500 : 1000,\n})\n```\n\nA changed `wait` value does not reschedule existing trailing work. It applies to later scheduling and executions. Disabling the throttler through `setOptions()` cancels pending trailing work.\n\nUse `asyncThrottlerOptions()` to define reusable, type-checked option objects.\n\n## State\n\nThe class stores state at `throttler.store`.\n\nTo restore selected state that your app has persisted, pass a partial snapshot through `initialState`. It is merged with the defaults. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers and active executions are not restored.\n\nCommonly useful properties include:\n\n- `isPending`: Whether a trailing execution is scheduled.\n- `isExecuting`: Whether the wrapped function is active.\n- `lastArgs`: The latest arguments retained for trailing work.\n- `lastResult`: The most recent successful result.\n- `lastExecutionTime` and `nextExecutionTime`: Current timing boundaries.\n- `successCount`, `errorCount`, and `settleCount`: Execution outcome counts.\n\nSee the [`AsyncThrottler` API reference](../../../reference/classes/AsyncThrottler.md) for the complete state and option types.\n"}
